## Interview Questions

### Basic Level

**Q1: What is Azure Data Factory and what problems does it solve?**

A: Azure Data Factory (ADF) is a fully managed, serverless data integration service that orchestrates data movement and transformation at scale. It solves several key problems:

```
Problems Solved:
├─ Complex data orchestration across systems
├─ Hybrid integration (on-premises and cloud)
├─ Scaling data pipelines without infrastructure
├─ Scheduling and monitoring workflows
├─ Data quality and error handling
├─ Cost optimization for data operations
└─ Enabling real-time data integration

Key Capabilities:
├─ Move data between 90+ sources/destinations
├─ Transform data using Spark (Data Flows)
├─ Orchestrate complex workflows
├─ Monitor and manage at scale
├─ Secure sensitive data
└─ Integrate with other Azure services
```

**Q2: Explain the difference between Azure IR, Self-Hosted IR, and SSIS IR.**

A:
```
Azure Integration Runtime:
├─ Default cloud runtime
├─ Managed by Azure (no maintenance)
├─ For cloud-to-cloud data movement
├─ Optimal performance for Azure services
├─ Auto-scaling and load balancing
└─ Pay per execution

Self-Hosted IR:
├─ Installed on customer machines
├─ For on-premises data sources
├─ Bidirectional data movement
├─ Custom transformation support
├─ Hybrid scenarios (on-prem ↔ cloud)
├─ Requires network connectivity
└─ Customer manages lifecycle

SSIS IR:
├─ Runs SSIS packages in cloud
├─ Lift-and-shift migration path
├─ Azure-managed SSIS servers
├─ Windows authentication support
├─ Scale-out deployment
└─ Legacy system modernization
```

**Q3: What are Linked Services and Datasets?**

A:
```
Linked Services:
├─ Connection information to external resources
├─ Define how to connect (SQL DB, Blob Storage)
├─ Store authentication credentials
├─ Configure retry and timeout policies
├─ Reference in activities
└─ Reusable across pipelines

Example:
{
  "type": "AzureSqlDatabase",
  "server": "servername.database.windows.net",
  "database": "mydb",
  "authentication": "Managed Identity"
}

Datasets:
├─ Represent physical data structure
├─ Reference a Linked Service
├─ Define schema and format
├─ Specify file path or table name
├─ Support multiple formats (CSV, Parquet, etc.)
├─ Enable data mapping
└─ Reusable across activities

Example:
{
  "name": "CustomerDataset",
  "type": "AzureSqlTable",
  "linkedServiceName": "SqlConnection",
  "typeProperties": {
    "table": "dbo.Customers",
    "schema": [
      {"name": "Id", "type": "Int32"},
      {"name": "Name", "type": "String"}
    ]
  }
}
```

**Q4: What is a Pipeline and what are common triggers?**

A: A Pipeline is a container for logical workflow of activities:

```
Pipeline Components:
├─ Activities (tasks to execute)
├─ Dependencies (execution order)
├─ Parameters (inputs)
├─ Variables (state management)
├─ Triggers (scheduling/execution)
└─ Timeout and retry policies

Common Triggers:

Schedule Trigger:
├─ Time-based execution
├─ Recurrence (hourly, daily, weekly)
├─ Specific times and timezones
